 - `--match-with-call-graph`
 
   Match functions with call graph
+
+- `--memcpy-short-profile=<file>`
+
+  Enable profile-guided specialization of x86-64 ELF tail calls to
+  `memcpy@PLT`. Use this option with the standard libc `memcpy` contract,
+  since rewritten calls bypass symbol interposition. This option is
+  incompatible with `--inline-memcpy`.
+
+  The first non-comment line is `bolt-memcpy-profile-v1 <sha256>`, where
+  `<sha256>` is the digest of the input ELF. Each subsequent record contains
+  the original hexadecimal instruction address, the decimal observation
+  count, and 17 decimal counts for lengths 0 through 16. Blank lines and
+  comment lines starting with `#` are skipped. At least 10,000 observations
+  and at least 90% lengths of 16 bytes or less are required for a rewrite.
+  Supply the regular execution profile as usual.
+
+- `--memcpy-reuse-length-branch`
+
+  With a length profile, recognize an existing full-width zero check that
+  controls a supported memcpy tail call. Reuse it to dispatch short copies.
+  Sites without a proven match are skipped in this mode.
+
+- `--memcpy-reuse-long-fallthrough`
+
+  When reordering basic blocks for a reused check, place the original memcpy
+  path immediately after the check so that it can fall through.
+
+- `--layout-probe-outline-reused-short`
+
+  Place the added short blocks in a separate fragment during function splitting.
+  The experimental driver `bolt/utils/bolt_safe_layout.py` pairs this with a
+  reference layout and audits the emitted addresses and bytes. It outputs the
+  off baseline if it cannot verify a unique eligible function.
 
 - `--memcpy1-spec=<func1,func2:cs1:cs2,func3:cs1,...>`
 
